---
/**
 * Terms of use.
 *
 * The parts that matter here are the disclaimer and the licensing, because this
 * site publishes other people's data under their licences and its own coding
 * under ours, and a reader needs to know which is which before reusing anything.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const updated = "13 September 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Terms"
  description="What you can do with what is published here, what is ours and what is not, and what this site does not promise."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>Terms</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">Last updated {updated}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          Use it freely. Check it before you rely on it. Nothing here is legal, financial,
          investment or professional advice of any kind.
        </p>

        <h2>This is not advice</h2>
        <p>
          The law and policy index in particular is a map of where to start reading, not a
          compliance product. It is a reading of public instruments at a review date, it does not
          account for your circumstances, and reasonable lawyers would code several entries
          differently. If a decision turns on it, get advice from someone qualified in the
          jurisdiction concerned.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same applies elsewhere. Governance readiness scores measure how much machinery a
          jurisdiction has, not whether its rules are good or well enforced. A high score is not a
          compliment and a low one is not an accusation.
        </p>

        <h2>No warranty</h2>
        <p>
          The site is provided as it is, with no warranty of any kind. Figures come from other
          people's datasets, which have their own errors, revisions and limitations, and we add
          errors of our own. Every figure carries its source, its licence and its retrieval date
          precisely so you can check it rather than trust it. Please do check it.
        </p>
        <p>
          To the extent the law allows, we accept no liability for any loss arising from use of
          this site or reliance on anything published here. Nothing in these terms limits rights
          you have under the Australian Consumer Law or equivalent legislation that cannot be
          excluded.
        </p>

        <h2>What is ours, and what you can do with it</h2>
        <p>
          The site's code, its written explanations, the Governance Readiness Index and the AI Law
          and Policy Index are ours, and are licensed
          <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. Take them, change
          them, build on them commercially, and credit us.
        </p>

        <h2>What is not ours</h2>
        <p>
          Most of the figures on this site are reproduced from other people's datasets under their
          own licences, and remain theirs. Those licences are not ours to widen. Before reusing a
          figure from here, check the licence on the
          <a href="/sources/">sources page</a> or in the provenance popover attached to the figure
          itself, and comply with that rather than with these terms.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two constraints come up often enough to state plainly. The AI Incident Database is
          licensed ODbL, which has share-alike conditions on derived databases. News headlines and
          extracts belong to their publishers; we show a headline, at most 200 characters and a
          link, which is what we consider defensible, and copying more of them from here is on
          you.
        </p>

        <h2>Linking and reuse of the site</h2>
        <p>
          Link to anything. Quote figures with attribution. Do not present the site or its data as
          endorsed by any of the publishers we cite: none of them endorse this site, and several
          of the licences we rely on say so explicitly.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections and takedowns</h2>
        <p>
          If something here is wrong, <a href="/corrections/">tell us</a>. If you hold rights in
          something published here and want it removed, tell us and we will remove it. The
          architecture is built for that: source data lives in one registry, and removing a source
          removes everything derived from it in a single change.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          These terms may change. Changes appear here, with the date at the top updated to match.
          Anything that changes what you may do with the material, rather than merely how it is
          worded, is also listed on the <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
